**Goal:** add one action and get a command, an HTTP route, an [MCP](/lore/mcp-is-another-surface) tool, an [RPC](/skills/learn-rat-stack) call, and a code-mode function from it.

A capability is one named action with a [contract](/systems/capabilities) and a server-side [handler](/systems/capabilities).
A contract defines the shared input, output, failures, and metadata.
[Effect](/lore/effect-basics) [Schema](/lore/schemas-define-the-boundary) provides types and runtime checks for these values. `defineContract` owns the name, Effect schemas, annotations, and approval setting. `implement` binds that contract to its handler.

Here, a surface is an [interface](/lore/one-schema-three-surfaces) for calling the action.
A projection is code that converts registered capabilities into an interface.
The interfaces are command line, HTTP, MCP tools, browser RPC, and [code mode](/lore/one-program-can-replace-several-tool-calls).
Each interface derives its input and failure definitions from the same schemas.

## Worked example: `inspectFile`

### 1. The contract

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/core/src/contracts.ts at=a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725 lines=292-300 title="Name, schemas, failure, and annotations"

```

The contract holds no handler. A browser can import it without server code.

### 2. The handler

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/core/src/inspect-file.ts at=a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725 lines=14-19 {14-16,19} title="implement binds the handler; capabilities registers it"

```

The handler runs [the inspection machine](/lore/lifecycles-are-machines).

### 3. The projections

```ts repo=rat-stack path=apps/cli/src/surfaces.ts at=a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725 lines=40-44 title="HTTP, MCP tools, and code mode from the same lists"

```

```ts repo=rat-stack path=apps/cli/src/command.ts at=a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725 lines=22-28 {25-28} title="The CLI command, with a stats alias"

```

No projection repeats the schema. Each one reads the contract it was given.

## Why one contract

A second hand-written route would need its own schema, and the two could drift.
Here each surface derives its input and failure from one place.

The split replaced `defineCapability`, which bundled contract data and its handler in one definition. The RPC [client](/skills/uncomplect) needed the contract without importing server implementation. `toRpcGroup` now projects contracts alone; `toRpc` connects those same contracts to implemented capabilities. The browser docs client imports only the shared contracts and RPC group. [Commit 9a7bea5](https://github.com/joelhooks/rat-stack/commit/9a7bea5) records the split.

Michael Arnaldi demonstrates Schema decoding request input and encoding a frontend-safe result while preserving its output type. [Effect Meetup SF, 27:17](https://youtu.be/BqUnsDnMnMo?t=1637). In another conversation, the speakers discuss generated schemas and migrations as work agents can maintain, and a rule that rejects ad hoc JSON parsing. [Cause & Effect 10, 63:49](https://youtu.be/irmz5X3c7Do?t=3829) [67:53](https://youtu.be/irmz5X3c7Do?t=4073).

## Next

To add a capability, define its contract and implement it beside the data or infrastructure it owns. Register it in `capabilities`, then check each interface you keep. `packages/capability/test/` exercises projections; `apps/site/src/capabilities/index.ts` shows the hosted registry.

Use [the add-a-capability skill](/skills/add-a-capability) for the steps.

[Analytics](/systems/analytics) shows a separate infrastructure [cartridge](/lore/cartridges); [interest signup](/systems/interest) shows core ports with a delivery [adapter](/lore/hexagonal-architecture) outside core. Neither turns static pages or protocol discovery into domain capabilities.

See [lifecycles are machines](/lore/lifecycles-are-machines) for stateful handlers and [cartridges](/lore/cartridges) for the infrastructure boundary. [HATEOAS](/lore/hateoas) covers links that guide an agent's next action; [hexagonal architecture](/lore/hexagonal-architecture) names the ports-and-adapters principle.

